From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 06:34:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: add a driver for the A10/A20 mixer processor

The A20 mixer processor (MP, "G2D" in the vendor kernel) is a 2D engine
that copies, fills and alpha-blends rectangles at about 130 Mpix/s
(copy, bound by DRAM bandwidth). On the A20 the X server presents GL
windows and copies areas with glamor on the Mali-400, where each copy
costs a full tile reload and write-back of the destination; offloading
these copies to MP frees the GPU and uses less DRAM bandwidth.

The driver is a render-only DRM device without allocation: buffers come
from other drivers (sun4i-drm dumb/scanout buffers) as dma-bufs through
PRIME import. The DMA GEM helper rejects anything that is not
contiguous in the DMA address space of MP, which is all the engine can
address, and the mapping made for MP carries its MBUS DMA offset. Since
a missing offset would make MP write 1 GiB away from the buffers on
boards with 2 GiB of DRAM, the probe checks the device's DMA range map
and every buffer's bus address is checked against its physical address.

One synchronous ioctl runs a batch of up to 256 rectangles of one
operation (copy, fill, or non-premultiplied source-over blend) between
two linear ARGB8888/XRGB8888 surfaces. It waits for the implicit fences
of the buffers first, validates every rectangle against the surface and
the object size before the engine is touched, and reports how many
rectangles were done if the engine fails mid-batch. The engine does no
cache maintenance, so the buffers must be write-combined, as sun4i-drm
buffers are. Overlapping copies inside one buffer need a scan order
that is not verified on hardware yet and are refused unless the
allow_overlap module parameter is set.

The engine is clocked and taken out of reset only while in use (runtime
PM with autosuspend), is reset after a timeout or an error, and is
disabled for good if it stays busy after a reset.

The register programming follows the linux-sunxi 3.4 sunxi_g2d driver
and the A20 user manual. Copy, fill and blend of ARGB8888 were verified
from user space through /dev/mem; the interrupt, the XRGB8888 paths and
the recovery are new in this driver.

Tested on a Rikomagic MK802 (A10) as well: verify, negative and overlap
tests pass.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
